## Context
Audit: `conception/2026-10-29-memory-system-audit.md` (PR #1984) §3.2, R1; Fable and GPT-6 Astra reviews of the fifth hoard (PR #1975, closed unmerged 2026-10-29 by author decision). Topics grew from 15 KB (2026-10-03) to 120 KB on the fifth hoard's branch:
dated "Later measurements" and "Refresh" sections, and superseded claims marked in place rather
than retired. Both reviewers estimated 10-35% of topic text states what is true now.
No document says what a topic is for, and only `MEMORY.md` has a budget (100 lines).
Structural clauses both reviewers named: `skills/hoard/SKILL.md:69-70` (quotations never
condensed or merged), `memory/HOARD.md` § Coherence and "Keep details in topics".

## Scope
- State the job of a topic in `memory/HOARD.md` and the hoard skill (an author decision; the
  lair session proposed a definition in its report of 2026-10-29).
- A per-topic byte cap and a total cap well under today's size. The cap binds on history, not on
  conditions and exceptions (ACE's brevity-bias warning).
- Superseded claims leave topics; the journal and git keep the history.
- Scope the verbatim-quotation clause to manuscript sources.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] HOARD.md and the hoard skill define the job of a topic and its caps
- [ ] A check reports topic sizes against the caps
- [ ] The quotation clause no longer applies to operational memory

## Proposal for the author's ruling (lair session, 2026-10-29)
Discussed with the author in the session that closed #1975; the author asked for it here.

**Theory of change.** Memory changes behaviour only when a current fact reaches the agent at
the decision point, in few enough words to be read. Every fact either becomes procedure (a rule,
a skill step, a test) and then leaves memory, or it expires. Consequences:
- memory is a waiting room for procedure, so a healthy store shrinks when brood promotes lessons;
- success is fewer repeated incidents, not more pages (measurable through 1346's trial);
- injection comes before content: pages nobody reads change nothing (1346 first).

**Fixed departments, the author's idea.** A fixed list of topics, each a stable path that a
delegation brief can name ("relevant memory topics by path", `rules/workflow.md` § Delegation).
Each has its own budget, and none may split by date. One history department holds the past:
the journal, hoard reports and git. Every other department holds the current state only, and a
superseded claim moves to history instead of being annotated in place. Each department also has
a fallback box for material that fits no department, which the next hoard empties.
Candidate departments for the harness:
worktrees and concurrency; merge and review; testing and gates; runtimes and model routing;
hosts and environment; tickets and state; external tools (Zotero, DOIfetch); memory itself;
history (not a topic).

**Organizing metaphor: the as-built file.** It matches the MOE/MOA posture vocabulary
(`rules/workflow.md` § Posture). The name "hoard" says the opposite: a hoard only grows.
| Construction | Harness |
|---|---|
| Journal de chantier | `memory/journal/` (history) |
| Lots (trades), a fixed list | topics, one per department |
| DOE (dossier des ouvrages exécutés): as built, current | the content of each topic |
| Cahier des charges, normes | `rules/`, skills, tests |
| Updating the DOE | hoard: rewrite each lot to match the as-built state |

**Page shape.** For each department page: current guidance (claims in the present tense, with
their scope), hazards, open questions, retired (one line per claim, naming the change that
retired it), and sources (journal links).

Open for the author: accept or amend the list of departments; whether to rename hoard;
the byte cap per department.

## Departments across projects: sweep of padme, 2026-10-29 (author request)
Projects with a memory store or tickets, excluding arena copies and workloads:
harness (`.agents`), arena (model bench), DOIfetch, git-erg, zoteus, search-works-for-zotero
(utility tools); padme (host administration); JETP-observer (data observatory);
climate-finance-het (corpus-driven research programme and monograph); sen-causal-reading,
fuzzy-corpus, cadens (articles); AEDIST-technical-report (report); chemin-de-voix (literary
translation book). Only three have topics. Existing topic names already cluster:
- author relationship: cfh `author-working-relationship`, JETP `author-working-preferences`,
  padme `working-in-this-repo`;
- delivery machinery: cfh `git-and-branches`, `tickets-and-decisions`, `orchestration-and-gates`,
  `guards-and-verification`, `build-and-pipeline`; harness ci, merge, worktrees; padme `deployment-and-systemd`;
- the object being made: cfh `corpus-store`, `rel-corpus`, `manuscripts-and-sources`; padme
  `gpu-driver-and-firmware`, `llm-backend`, `monitoring-architecture`.
Review-attribution records fill the journals everywhere: cfh 75/193, JETP 37/65, padme 8/13,
sen-causal-reading 12/45, arena 7/49, DOIfetch 2/4. Ticket 1347 fixes this for every project,
since roar is a harness skill.

Proposed scheme: a fixed core shared by every project, plus a fixed set of departments for each
type of work (the "ouvrage"). A project combines types (cfh: analysis + book; fuzzy-corpus: article + tool).
The total stays at 8 departments or fewer per project.

Core, every project:
1. Commission: the author's intent, rulings and working preferences (MOA side).
2. Site practice: how work lands here (branches, tickets, gates, build). Only local deviations
   from the harness; harness machinery is recorded once, in the harness.
3. Environment: hosts, services, external tools and their quirks.
4. History: journal, hoard reports and git; never a topic page.

Ouvrage departments, by type:
| Type | Examples | Departments |
|---|---|---|
| Tool / code | harness, DOIfetch, git-erg, zoteus, arena | architecture and contracts; interfaces and compatibility (runtimes, users); release and distribution |
| Analysis / data | JETP-observer, cfh corpus, arena results | data and provenance; methods and measurement; results and their limits |
| Article / report | sen-causal-reading, fuzzy-corpus, AEDIST, cadens | argument and claims; literature and sources; venue and submission |
| Book | cfh monograph, chemin-de-voix | structure and chapters; voice and editorial decisions; sources, rights and permissions |
| Host / infrastructure | padme | services and deployment; hardware; access and privileged operations; monitoring |

For the harness, the machinery is the product: merge, review, worktrees and testing belong under
its ouvrage (architecture and contracts), and a consumer project's site practice page only records
its deviations. Consumer projects do not re-record harness behaviour; a harness defect becomes a
harness ticket (`rules/workflow.md` § Cross-project work).

## Author rulings, 2026-10-29
- Departments: the core-plus-type scheme above is accepted **provisionally**; the 1349 rebuild
  tests it on the harness before other projects adopt it.
- Rename: hoard becomes **smelt** (ticket 1352).
- Still open: the byte cap per department.

## Two borrowings from other designs (author, 2026-10-29)
- **Pinned rulings.** Qwen Code keeps `pinned/` notes that consolidation never rewrites; only an
  explicit forget removes them. Commission rulings get the same status: smelt may file, date and
  link them, never paraphrase or retire them; only the author retires a ruling.
- **Recall with its source.** Le Chat shows when a memory is used and links it to its source.
  Every department claim carries its source link (journal entry, note, PR), and an agent that acts
  on a memory claim cites it in its report.

## Journal as source, departments as guide (author, 2026-10-29)
Two independent results say consolidation must prove itself against raw episodes:
DreamBench-SWE (1346: a rich typed consolidation pipeline did not beat verbatim event memory,
95/180 versus 89/180, with worse useful-memory precision, 0.264 versus 0.556, and worse staleness
handling, 8/21 versus 13/21) and "Useful Memories Become Faulty" (May 2026, in the audit:
repeated LLM rewriting can fall below no memory, while kept raw episodes hold up).

Consequences for the department scheme:
- The journal is the source of truth for what happened, and it stays intact: append-only, never
  rewritten or condensed by smelt, and searchable. Attribution records leave it (1347), but no
  episode does.
- A department page is a guide to the journal, not a replacement for it. It holds the current
  guidance in a few lines, and each claim links to the episodes that support it. An agent that
  needs the detail follows the link to the raw entry instead of trusting a paraphrase.
- Smelt rewrites the guides, never the sources. When a guide claim and its source episode
  disagree, the episode wins, and the claim is corrected or retired.
- The value a department adds is cheaper reading (finding the right episodes and knowing what is
  current), not better recall. 1346's verbatim-journal control arm tests exactly this: if the
  guides do not beat raw journal retrieval on reading cost or on stale-fact use, shrink them.

## Author ruling: the cap (2026-10-29), option B
- **4 KB per department page** (about 60 lines), measured in bytes, which track reading cost,
  not in lines. The cap covers the guide sections: current guidance, hazards and open questions.
  Across eight departments that totals about 32 KB; a delegate reading two or three pages reads
  about 12 KB (roughly 4,000 tokens).
- The "Retired" list (one line per retired claim) is outside the cap and moves to history at the
  next smelt.
- Pinned Commission rulings count against the cap but are never cut to fit. An overflowing
  Commission page signals rules waiting for the author to promote them (brood, 1351).
- The `MEMORY.md` index gets its own cap of 40 lines: one line per department, with its most-used fact.
- The cap ratchets down after 1346. If the verbatim-journal control arm matches the guides on
  stale-fact use and reading cost, move toward pointer-only pages (about 25 lines each).
Rejected: 200 lines per page (Claude Code's index load limit); with eight pages that reproduces
today's 1,766 lines.
With this ruling, the open questions of this ticket are settled: departments (provisional),
rename (smelt, 1352), and cap.

## Imagine (raid b7c67c)
Premise check against the tree (2026-10-29): the ticket's `HOARD.md` and hoard skill are now
`memory/SMELT.md` (1352, prompt revision v8-r2) and `skills/smelt/SKILL.md`; the quotation clause
is `skills/smelt/SKILL.md:69-71` ("Carry quotations verified on the page ... verbatim"), and
SMELT.md has no such clause. SMELT.md has no "Keep details in topics" either; the skill has it
(line 88). Attribution records are already out of the journal path (1347), so SMELT.md's legacy
carve-out stays only until 1349 confirms no project still holds some. Today: 11 topic files,
about 85 KB, largest `raid-review-and-merge.md` 21 KB; index 37 lines. Only "100 lines" is pinned
anywhere (`tests/test_dream_v8.py:92`, SMELT.md, the skill twice, `AGENTS.md:75`).
No topic-size check exists. `scripts/resident_census.py` budgets the resident index in chars; the
topics are not resident, so extending it would mix two jobs.

Simplest path to the three exit criteria, no new machinery:
1. Prose (criteria 1 and 3): one short "Job and caps" section in SMELT.md, a pointer plus the
   quotation scoping in the skill. The skill stays procedure, SMELT.md holds the editorial rule.
2. Check (criterion 2): one ~40-line script `scripts/memory_sizes.py` that prints bytes per
   topic page against the cap, plus one pytest file calling its function. Not a census extension,
   not a hook, no guard on the guard: the pytest is the check's own regression, nothing more.
3. Transition: 1349 rebuilds the pages, so on 1348's merge the real topics are all over 4 KB.
   Use the repo's own ratchet idiom (`tests/test_resident_census.py` BUDGETS at measured value):
   a `TRANSITION` dict of per-file ceilings at today's sizes, deleted by 1349. Cheaper than
   skipping the real tree, and the check never reports a false all-clear.

Price: about 1 coder-hour, about 25k tokens. Probability the cap definition survives 1349
unchanged: about 60%; the likely amendment is where "Sources" counts (below).

Decisions the text must make, which the rulings leave unstated (flag to the orchestrator):
- D1. What the cap measures. The ruling: guide sections (current guidance, hazards, open
  questions) count; Retired is outside. Sources is unnamed. Proposal: every H2 section counts
  except the one titled `Retired`; Sources counts, because source links are claim-bearing
  and "recall with source" would otherwise be a way to grow past the cap. Reversible in one line.
- D2. Commission overflow. "Never cut to fit" means the check cannot fail the page. It prints
  `OVER (promote to rules, brood 1351)` and exits 0 for that page only; any other page over cap fails.
- D3. `AGENTS.md:75` says "100 lines". It is an instruction file; the author's call whether the
  smelt session edits it. Without it, two documents contradict (40 versus 100).
- D4. Pinned rulings are not machine-detectable without a marker. Proposal: a line starting
  `> pinned` inside the Commission page. The check does not count them separately; no new rule.

## Plan (raid b7c67c)
Dependencies: 1352 (done: SMELT.md and skill names are current). 1346's ratchet is later and is
not this ticket. 1349 consumes the definitions and deletes the TRANSITION dict.

Actions, in order, by file:
1. `memory/SMELT.md`
   - New section `## Job of a topic and caps`, after `## Read`: a topic is the as-built
     (DOE) guide to one department, present tense, each claim with its scope and a source link
     to a journal episode; page shape (guidance, hazards, open questions, Retired, Sources); the
     journal stays the intact source and the episode wins over a guide claim; pinned Commission
     rulings are filed, dated and linked, never paraphrased or retired by smelt; cap 4 KB (4096
     bytes) per page measured on all sections except Retired; Retired moves to history at the next
     smelt; a Commission overflow is reported, not cut; the departments list (core + per-type,
     provisional) in a table or one list; fallback box per page, emptied each smelt.
   - `## Bounds`: "at most 100 lines" becomes 40 (headings and blanks counted); one line per
     department with its most-used fact. Bump `Prompt revision` to v8-r3 with today's date.
   - `## Consolidate` Merging bullet: add "Quotations from manuscript sources keep their
     wording and locators; operational memory is condensed freely." (the clause lives there,
     since SMELT.md is the editorial layer.)
   - `## Report`: one line, "the bytes of each topic page against the cap" (1349 adds the net change).
2. `skills/smelt/SKILL.md`
   - Lines 69-71 (Merging bullet): scope "Carry quotations verified on the page and their
     locators" to "quotations from manuscript sources"; drop "never paraphrase, condense or merge
     them away" for operational memory; keep the conflicting-locator sentence under the same scope.
   - Line 88 and procedure step 2 and 4: "100 lines" becomes 40, three sites; "Keep details in
     topics" becomes "keep details in the journal, linked from the department page".
   - One new bullet under "Three main functions": a topic's job and the 4 KB cap, one sentence
     each, then "details: `memory/SMELT.md` § Job of a topic and caps" (no restatement of the table).
   - Run `make skills-catalog` only if the description changes; it should not.
3. `scripts/memory_sizes.py` (new, stdlib only): `guide_bytes(text)` returns the UTF-8 byte
   count of the page minus every `## Retired` section (heading to next H2 or EOF);
   `report(topics_dir)` yields (name, bytes, cap, status); `main()` prints a table and exits 1 on
   any non-Commission page over cap. `CAP = 4096`; `INDEX_LINES = 40`. Index lines counted with
   the same rule as SMELT.md (every line). Filename for Commission: `commission.md` (provisional
   per the ruling; 1349 may rename it, one constant).
4. `tests/test_memory_sizes.py` (new), `pytestmark = pytest.mark.adherence` as in the census test.
5. `tests/test_dream_v8.py:92`: `"100 lines"` becomes `"40 lines"` (the prompt pin follows the prompt).
6. `AGENTS.md:75`: D3, orchestrator to decide; do not edit unasked.

First failing test (written before the script, red for the right reason):
`test_retired_section_is_outside_the_cap`: a synthetic page of 3 KB guidance plus a 6 KB
`## Retired` passes; the same page with a 4.2 KB guidance and an empty Retired is OVER. A
implementation that counts the whole file fails the first case; one that strips everything after
the first H2 fails the second. Companion cases in the same file, each one assertion:
`test_commission_over_cap_warns_not_fails`; `test_exact_cap_passes_and_cap_plus_one_fails`
(bytes, with a multibyte character so a character count fails); `test_index_line_cap` (a
41-line index fails, 40 passes, blanks counted); `test_real_tree_is_measured` (positive control:
at least 10 pages found, so a glob that matches nothing cannot pass; every page at or under its
TRANSITION ceiling, each ceiling equal to today's size, so growth fails and a rebuild passes).
Exit criterion 3: `test_quotation_clause_scoped`: skill text contains "manuscript" within the
quotation sentence and no longer contains "never paraphrase, condense or merge them away" unqualified.

Verification: `python3 scripts/memory_sizes.py` on the tree must list all 11 pages, flag
`raid-review-and-merge.md` as over the 4 KB cap, and exit 0 under TRANSITION; run the scoped gate
(`.idh-checks.json` if present) and quote selected/skipped. 1349 is not started here.

## Orchestrator decisions (raid b7c67c)
- D1: a Sources section sits outside the 4 KB cap, like Retired. The author's ruling lists the
  capped sections as current guidance, hazards and open questions. Inline source links inside
  claims count, since they belong to the claim.
- D2: a Commission page over the cap warns and never fails, as the ruling says
  ("never cut to fit").
- D3: `AGENTS.md:75` ("Keep MEMORY.md at most 100 lines") is changed to 40 lines in this PR. This
  carries out the author's explicit ruling of 2026-10-29 (index cap 40 lines) and is named in the
  PR body for the author.
- D4: pinned rulings are marked by a `> pinned` line, as proposed.
